Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero 0.7.0 IA32 target

compiler.zero is written in Zero and runs on Linux x86-64. Its IA32 target
emits native i386 machine code directly. It uses no assembler, linker,
foreign compiler backend, guest runtime, OS stub or
arithmetic library. The protected-mode kernel remains kernel.zero, shared
with the x86-64 build. Its disk format and u64 generation/checksum semantics
therefore remain the same on both CPU modes.

Build the compiler and system
bash build.sh

Targets
build/zero --raw32 < kernel.zero > build/kernel32.bin
build/zero --efi32 < loader32.zero > build/BOOTIA32.EFI
The IA32 target requires an explicit option; the compiler defaults to Linux
x86-64 ELF. Linux put/exit/read/write/pread/pwrite/fsync operations are not supported in an
IA32 target. The compiler runs
on a Linux x86-64 build host; the emitted code runs on an IA32 target.

Grammar and values
The same current Zero syntax is used: value => @name. declarations, value
-> @name. assignments, @references, ? condition [ ... ] ~ [ ... ] branches,
?? condition [ ... ] loops, :operation[arguments separated by |] expressions,
and !operation[arguments]. effects. Argument-free effects end with a period.
See LANGUAGE.ztext for the shared grammar. Legacy var/if/while/C-style calls
and brace/semicolon syntax are not accepted.

Values remain unsigned u64, including on the 32-bit target. Arithmetic wraps
modulo2^64; comparisons are unsigned and produce0 or1. The backend represents
a value in EDX:EAX, with EAX holding the low32bits. Addition and subtraction
propagate carry/borrow, multiplication computes the complete low64bits, and
comparison examines the high32bits before the low32bits. A condition is true
if either half is nonzero. There is no implicit conversion to signed values.
:bitxor[left | right] and :bitand[left | right] combine both32-bit halves.
:shr[value | count] returns the logical u64 right shift. Counts0..31 use
cross-word shifting; counts32..63 use the original high word as the low result.
Counts>=64, including any nonzero high count word, return0. This matches x64
semantics and does not truncate counts to the hardware's low5 bits.

Memory and I/O
load8/16/32 zero-extend to u64; load64 reads both little-endian halves.
store8/16/32 write the low requested bits; store64 writes both halves.
64-bit accesses are two IA32 operations and are not atomic. The current
kernel switches cooperatively on one CPU with interrupts disabled, so no
worker interrupts another between those two operations. This does not supply
cross-CPU atomicity or SMP synchronization. Memory addresses must fit32bits; a nonzero high half
traps with UD2. There are no general pointer bounds or exception recovery.
Port I/O uses the low16 port bits and low8/16 value bits as on x86-64.

reserve uses the same64KiB arena and16-byte alignment. A size with nonzero
high32bits, rounding overflow, address wrap or arena exhaustion traps with
UD2. The private frame contains64 eight-byte variable slots, saved entry
arguments and an arena cursor. Generated code needs at least80KiB below its
entry stack pointer. Both the BIOS and IA32 UEFI loader provide a larger
dedicated kernel stack. No SSE, MMX, floating-point, PAE or long mode is
required by the generated kernel. CPU/platform boot requirements are separate.

Entry and firmware ABI
Raw32 and EFI32 both use the IA32 C entry layout: a return address at ESP,
arg0 at ESP+4, arg1 at ESP+8. Initial arguments are32bit values zero-extended
to Zero u64 and preserved in private frame slots. The raw kernel receives
its handoff pointer as arg0. These top-level Zero programs never return.

:call[function | a1 | a2 | a3 | a4 | a5] supplies five32bit stack words.
:call7[function | a1 | a2 | a3 | a4 | a5 | a6 | a7] supplies seven words.
These are explicit IA32 ABI interfaces: each argument contributes its low
32bits, not a full source-language u64. The function address must fit32bits.
Arguments are evaluated left-to-right, put on the target stack in C order,
and ESP is16-byte aligned immediately before CALL. The callee must preserve
EBX, ESI, EDI and EBP according to IA32 C ABI; EAX/ECX/EDX are volatile. The
caller restores its stack and returns EAX zero-extended to u64. Thus an
EFI_STATUS error has bit31 set, not bit63. Other 64-bit-return C ABIs are not
implemented by these firmware-call primitives.

UEFI functions with by-value UINT64 arguments need explicit word expansion.
EFI_FILE_PROTOCOL.Open is called with seven words: This, NewHandle, Path,
OpenMode low, OpenMode high, Attributes low, Attributes high. call7 handles
this without hiding file policy in the compiler. SetWatchdogTimer consumes
five words because its WatchdogCode is UINT64. loader32.zero implements the
firmware protocol and structure offsets in Zero source.

!enter[entry | arg | stack_top]. disables interrupts, installs an aligned
stack, provides a halt-return address, arg0=arg and arg1=0, and jumps to the
entry. All three values must fit32bits. It provides only the architectural
entry ABI; allocation, display setup and ExitBootServices remain loader code.

Native cooperative threads
:fork[@context | @stack_base | @stack_bytes] and
!switch[@from_context | @to_context]. have the same source contract as x64.
The child copies the complete 512-byte u64 variable frame and live operands,
receives a fresh 64 KiB arena and resumes with fork result 1; the parent
receives 0. The private frame header holds arg0, arg1, arena cursor and
baseline at offsets 0,4,8,12. Full u64 variable values remain intact.
Context records are 80 bytes, aligned to 8; IA32 writes zero in high halves
of the native-state fields. Child stacks need 16-byte alignment, at least
81920 bytes and valid nonoverlapping ownership as detailed in LANGUAGE.ztext.
Existing pointer values remain shared. These primitives provide stack and
continuation mechanics, while all scheduling policy is Zero source.

PE32 output
Machine014c, optional-header size224/magic010b, subsystem10, two sections,
4096-byte section alignment and512-byte file alignment. Both code and
embedded literal addresses are position-independent. The relocation table
contains a valid no-op ABSOLUTE block so firmware can load at another base.
No import table or linked guest runtime is present. File metadata and code
remain within the compiler's393216-byte overall output bound; the image
builder additionally enforces its smaller per-artifact slots.

Verification
An actual BIOS32 boot under QEMU with long mode and PAE disabled reached the
shared kernel and mounted the existing ZeroFS disk. A generated regression
program then passed wide arithmetic, memory, control flow, string and entry
ABI checks, emitting exactly ABCDEFGHIJKLMN0123OP followed by LF before using
QEMU's test-only exit port. That port is not part of the production kernel.
See the release verification report for actual IA32 UEFI integration checks,
compiler rejection cases, platform limits and exact delivered artifact hashes.
Independent0.7 tests also passed110 bit/shift assertions under IA32 with long
mode and PAE disabled; see verification/compiler-fs/values/ia32-vm.

Dword port I/O
:in32[port] and !out32[port | value]. are available on raw32/EFI32 as well as
x64 and real mode. Both use the low16 port bits. Input clears EDX after the
32-bit read, producing the full-u64 zero-extended result; output uses the
low32 value bits. No PAE or long mode is required. See selfhost/port32.ztext.
